      "content": "interesting. so you're saying the actual content—the 15 years of experience and the specific skills—doesn't really matter as much as the way it's phrased?\n\nlike, if a candidate in Text B actually has a genius-level track record but just communicates in that style, are you saying they'd still be perceived as having low intelligence or competence in a high-level interview? i'm curious if you think the \"uneducated\" vibe is strictly about the grammar or if there's something else about that specific way of speaking that makes it feel less \"executive.\"",
      "source": "evaluator"
